=E2=86=90 All Posts</a></header><div class=3D"post-entry__products"><div cl=
ass=3D"post-entry__products-label">Products mentioned in this post</div><di=
v class=3D"post-entry__products-item"><a href=3D"https://2389.ai/products/m=
ammoth/" class=3D"post-entry__products-link">Mammoth</a>
<span class=3D"post-entry__products-desc">=E2=80=94 DOT-based pipeline runn=
er that turns directed graphs into multi-stage AI coding workflows with che=
ckpointing, retry, and human-in-the-loop gates.</span></div><div class=3D"p=
ost-entry__products-item"><a href=3D"https://2389.ai/products/smasher/" cla=
ss=3D"post-entry__products-link">Smasher</a>
<span class=3D"post-entry__products-desc">=E2=80=94 Rust pipeline runner th=
at turns DOT directed graphs into multi-step AI workflows with streaming, a=
 web dashboard, and human-in-the-loop gates.</span></div><div class=3D"post=
-entry__products-item"><a href=3D"https://2389.ai/products/tracker/" class=
=3D"post-entry__products-link">Tracker</a>
<span class=3D"post-entry__products-desc">=E2=80=94 Pipeline orchestration =
engine that runs DAG workflows from DOT files with human gates, LLM nodes, =
and automatic checkpointing.</span></div><div class=3D"post-entry__products=
-item"><a href=3D"https://2389.ai/products/dotpowers/" class=3D"post-entry_=
_products-link">dotpowers</a>
<span class=3D"post-entry__products-desc">=E2=80=94 The superpowers dev met=
hodology as a DOT pipeline =E2=80=94 brainstorms your idea, plans with TDD,=
 builds it with multi-model review, and ships.</span></div></div><div class=
=3D"post-entry__content prose prose-lg article-content"><p>So StrongDM publ=
ished a natural language spec for building a coding agent pipeline runner. =
Dan Shapiro built one. We built three. All of them =E2=80=94 independently,=
 in two languages, by different people with different goals =E2=80=94 lande=
d on the same three-layer architecture.</p><p>I keep coming back to that. N=
ot the code. The convergence. That=E2=80=99s the weird part.</p><h2 id=3D"t=
he-attractor-pattern">The attractor pattern</h2><p>In February, StrongDM op=
en-sourced <a href=3D"https://github.com/strongdm/attractor">attractor</a>:=
 three natural language specs describing a unified LLM client, a coding age=
nt loop, and a DOT-based pipeline engine. The specs aren=E2=80=99t code. Th=
ey=E2=80=99re prose. About 5,700 lines of it. Detailed enough that you can =
hand them to a coding agent and say =E2=80=9Cbuild this.=E2=80=9D And it wi=
ll.</p><p>The name is borrowed from dynamical systems =E2=80=94 an attracto=
r is a state a system tends to evolve toward. StrongDM=E2=80=99s bet is tha=
t these specs describe a design so natural for the problem that independent=
 implementations will converge on it. Bold claim! But uh, that=E2=80=99s ex=
actly what happened.</p><p>They also released <a href=3D"https://github.com=
/strongdm/attractorbench">AttractorBench</a>, which is a benchmark for meas=
uring how well coding agents implement systems from natural language specs.=
 It=E2=80=99s tiered =E2=80=94 smoke test, then a unified LLM SDK, then a c=
oding agent loop, then the full pipeline runner. Language-agnostic. Agents =
pick their own implementation language. The only contract is <code>make bui=
ld</code>, <code>make test</code>, and a conformance suite against a mock L=
LM server. No real API calls. Deterministic verification. Cost-aware scorin=
g. It doesn=E2=80=99t just ask =E2=80=9Cdid you build it?=E2=80=9D It asks =
=E2=80=9Chow well did you follow the spec, and what did it cost?=E2=80=9D</=
p><p>Dan Shapiro had been thinking about this progression for a while. In J=
anuary he published <a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com/blog/2026/01/the-=
five-levels-from-spicy-autocomplete-to-the-software-factory/">=E2=80=9CThe =
Five Levels: from Spicy Autocomplete to the Dark Factory=E2=80=9D</a>, borr=
owing the NHTSA=E2=80=99s driving automation levels for AI-assisted coding.=
 Level 0 is vi. No AI. Every character yours. Level 2 is where most =E2=80=
=9CAI-native=E2=80=9D developers are living right now =E2=80=94 pair-progra=
mming with a model, feeling productive. Level 4 is where you=E2=80=99ve bec=
ome a PM. You write specs, argue about specs, leave for 12 hours, check if =
the tests pass.</p><p>Level 5 is the dark factory. Lights off. Nobody revie=
ws the code. Nobody even looks at it.</p><p>The term =E2=80=9Cdark factory=
=E2=80=9D comes from manufacturing =E2=80=94 a factory run by robots where =
the lights are off because robots don=E2=80=99t need to see. Specifically F=
anuc Robotics in Japan around ~2003.</p><p>Applied to software, it=E2=80=99=
s kind of chilling and kind of exciting in equal measure.</p><p>After Stron=
gDM=E2=80=99s demo, Shapiro wrote <a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com/blo=
g/2026/02/you-dont-write-the-code/">=E2=80=9CYou Don=E2=80=99t Write the Co=
de. You Don=E2=80=99t Read the Code Either.=E2=80=9D</a> and then went and =
built <a href=3D"https://github.com/danshapiro/kilroy">Kilroy</a>. Local-fi=
rst Go CLI, runs attractor pipelines in isolated git worktrees, uses CXDB f=
or run history and checkpoint recovery. Another independent build. Same thr=
ee layers.</p><h2 id=3D"dorodango-or-why-we-built-three">Dorodango, or: why=
 we built three</h2><p>Jesse Vincent wrote a <a href=3D"https://blog.fsck.c=
om/2026/02/10/dorodango/">blog post about dorodango</a> =E2=80=94 the Japan=
ese art of polishing a ball of mud into a high-gloss sphere. Wikipedia=E2=
=80=99s disambiguation note for =E2=80=9Cmud ball=E2=80=9D redirects to =E2=
=80=9CBig Ball of Mud,=E2=80=9D the software anti-pattern. Jesse leaned int=
o it. I love this framing.</p><p>His point: codegen software is disposable.=
 You spec it carefully, hand it to an agent, polish what comes out. When th=
e result is fundamentally wrong, you don=E2=80=99t debug your way to salvat=
ion. You throw it away and rebuild from the spec. He described waking up to=
 find an agent=E2=80=99s end-to-end test recording named <code>e2e-test-ful=
l-run-33.mp4</code>. Runs 1 through 32 were the agent working through probl=
ems one by one. Run 33 worked. Pretty cool.</p><p>This is the mental model =
that let us build three attractor implementations without thinking twice ab=
out it. Software is cheap now. Specs are the expensive part.</p><p><a href=
=3D"https://github.com/2389-research/mammoth">Mammoth</a> and <a href=3D"ht=
tps://github.com/2389-ai/smasher">Smasher</a> were built in parallel from t=
he same spec. Mammoth, in Go, scope-crept in the best possible way =E2=80=
=94 it grew a 21-rule DOT linter, fan-in nodes with configurable join polic=
ies (all-success, majority, first-success), verification nodes that run she=
ll commands at zero token cost, and a 5-phase node lifecycle. It became thi=
s whole spec engine thing. Really cool, but also really big. Smasher, in Ru=
st, stayed lean: five crates from LLM client to web dashboard, an HTMX fron=
tend with live SSE streaming and graph visualization, six built-in agent to=
ols, and a <code>smasher chat</code> REPL for when you just want to talk to=
 the thing. Smasher is the one that actually gets used day-to-day.</p><p><a=
 href=3D"https://github.com/2389-research/tracker">Tracker</a> came later. =
Simpler. Go, bubbletea TUI, automatic checkpointing to <code>.tracker/runs/=
</code>, retry with backoff. A weekend-scale implementation that still conv=
erges on the same shape.</p><p>Because they all do. Every single one of the=
se =E2=80=94 Kilroy, Mammoth, Smasher, Tracker =E2=80=94 ends up with three=
 layers:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Kilroy (Go)</th><th>Mammoth=
 (Go)</th><th>Smasher (Rust)</th><th>Tracker (Go)</th></tr></thead><tbody><=
tr><td>LLM Client</td><td>Provider adapters</td><td><code>llm/</code> =E2=
=80=94 unified OpenAI/Anthropic/Gemini</td><td><code>smasher-llm</code> =E2=
=80=94 streaming, retries, provider quirks</td><td>Provider client with tra=
ce introspection</td></tr><tr><td>Agent Loop</td><td>Coding agent with tool=
 dispatch</td><td><code>agent/</code> =E2=80=94 steering, loop detection, s=
ubagents</td><td><code>smasher-agent</code> =E2=80=94 6 tools, steering rul=
es, subagents</td><td>LLM-powered nodes with context injection</td></tr><tr=
><td>Pipeline Engine</td><td>DOT parser, CXDB checkpoints, worktree isolati=
on</td><td><code>attractor/</code> =E2=80=94 DOT parser, graph engine, node=
 handlers</td><td><code>smasher-attractor</code> =E2=80=94 winnow parser, t=
okio broadcast</td><td>DAG walker, checkpointing, human gates</td></tr></tb=
ody></table><p>Nobody coordinated this. The spec pulled them there. That=E2=
=80=99s the attractor.</p><h2 id=3D"the-pipelines-are-the-product">The pipe=
lines are the product</h2><p>Ok so here=E2=80=99s the thing that=E2=80=99s =
been bugging me. The factory implementations are open source and multiplyin=
g. Great. But the pipeline files =E2=80=94 the DOT graphs that describe wha=
t the factory actually builds =E2=80=94 are mostly private. Everyone=E2=80=
=99s sharing the engine and hiding the blueprints.</p><blockquote><p>one qu=
ick clarification - for my entire life a dotfile was .bashrc, or a .vim or =
whatever. we are talking about a graphviz .dot file. I first learned about =
it from Justin when he first showed me his factory. It is the grandparent o=
f mermaid, sorta.</p></blockquote><p>A pipeline DOT file is a reusable blue=
print. It describes the workflow: which steps need an LLM, which need a hum=
an gate, where to fork into parallel branches, what verification commands t=
o run before proceeding. Standard Graphviz syntax. Nothing proprietary. And=
 honestly? The pipelines are way more interesting than the runners.</p><p>W=
e=E2=80=99ve been writing a lot of these, and two very different styles hav=
e emerged.</p><p>Here=E2=80=99s the first =E2=80=94 a vulnerability analyze=
r (<a href=3D"https://github.com/2389-research/tracker/blob/main/examples/v=
ulnerability_analyzer.dot"><code>vulnerability_analyzer.dot</code></a>) fro=
m Tracker=E2=80=99s examples:</p><figure><a href=3D"https://2389.ai/posts/t=
he-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/vuln-analyzer.png"><picture><source ty=
pe=3D"image/avif" srcset=3D"/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/vu=
ln-analyzer_hu_8a87ecb5be5b5c60.png 600w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-=
file/graphs/vuln-analyzer_hu_d5b9d9d1a811124.png 900w, /posts/the-dark-fact=
ory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/vuln-analyzer_hu_e6faa41ab1b357f8.png 1200w" sizes=
=3D"min(100vw, 800px)"><source type=3D"image/webp" srcset=3D"/posts/the-dar=
k-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/vuln-analyzer_hu_d31d888f123ea811.webp 600w,=
 /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/vuln-analyzer_hu_60e8549362f7=
9a50.webp 900w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/vuln-analyzer_=
hu_275d29a8a56c12b.webp 1200w" sizes=3D"min(100vw, 800px)"><img src=3D"http=
s://2389.ai/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/vuln-analyzer_hu_16=
16c349a87cb42d.png" alt=3D"Vulnerability analyzer pipeline =E2=80=94 all to=
ol nodes, no LLM calls" width=3D"1200" height=3D"81" loading=3D"lazy" decod=
ing=3D"async"></picture></a><figcaption>Vulnerability analyzer pipeline =E2=
=80=94 all tool nodes, no LLM calls</figcaption></figure><pre tabindex=3D"0=
" style=3D"position: relative;"><code class=3D"language-dot" data-lang=3D"d=
ot">digraph VulnerabilityAnalyzer {
  graph [
    goal=3D"Run a deterministic static vulnerability scan against a known
          vulnerable application and emit a report with evidence.",
    rankdir=3DLR,
    default_max_retry=3D1
  ];

  Start [shape=3DMdiamond];
  Exit  [shape=3DMsquare];

  CloneTarget [
    shape=3Dparallelogram,
    label=3D"Clone vulnerable target",
    tool_command=3D"set -eu
      mkdir -p .ai/vuln
      git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/digininja/DVWA.git .ai/vuln/ta=
rget
      printf 'ready'"
  ];

  StaticScan [
    shape=3Dparallelogram,
    label=3D"Run static scan",
    tool_command=3D"set -eu
      rg -n 'mysql_query\\(|eval\\(|shell_exec\\(' .ai/vuln/target &gt; .ai=
/vuln/findings.txt
      printf 'scanned'"
  ];

  WriteReport [
    shape=3Dparallelogram,
    label=3D"Write vulnerability report",
    tool_command=3D"set -eu
      count=3D$(wc -l &lt; .ai/vuln/findings.txt)
      echo \"# Report\" &gt; .ai/vuln/report.md
      echo \"Finding count: $count\" &gt;&gt; .ai/vuln/report.md
      printf 'report_written'"
  ];

  Start -&gt; CloneTarget -&gt; StaticScan -&gt; WriteReport -&gt; Exit;
}
</code><button class=3D"copy-btn" data-tinylytics-event=3D"code.copy" aria-=
label=3D"Copy code"><svg width=3D"14" height=3D"14" viewBox=3D"0 0 24 24" f=
ill=3D"none" stroke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=3D"2"><rect x=3D"9" y=3D"=
9" width=3D"13" height=3D"13" rx=3D"2" ry=3D"2"></rect><path d=3D"M5 15H4a2=
 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"></path></svg></button></pre=
><p>Every node is a <code>tool_command</code> =E2=80=94 just a shell script=
. No LLM calls. No token cost. Deterministic, reproducible, runs in seconds=
. The graph <em>is</em> the program. It rules.</p><p>Now compare that to th=
e other style, from Mammoth=E2=80=99s examples. This is <a href=3D"https://=
github.com/2389-research/mammoth/blob/main/examples/old/build_pong.dot"><co=
de>build_pong.dot</code></a>, a pipeline that builds a Pong game:</p><figur=
e><a href=3D"https://2389.ai/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/bu=
ild-pong.png"><picture><source type=3D"image/avif" srcset=3D"/posts/the-dar=
k-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/build-pong_hu_cc86c19740f11cc0.png 600w, /po=
sts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/build-pong_hu_e51b5dd230c278e0.pn=
g 900w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/build-pong_hu_cb814040=
96aad0a2.png 1200w" sizes=3D"min(100vw, 800px)"><source type=3D"image/webp"=
 srcset=3D"/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/build-pong_hu_7c26f=
68232041d90.webp 600w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/build-p=
ong_hu_8d1f671e9aa98d0f.webp 900w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/gr=
aphs/build-pong_hu_6568c57e8f928b8.webp 1200w" sizes=3D"min(100vw, 800px)">=
<img src=3D"https://2389.ai/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/bui=
ld-pong_hu_58b24e436f3eb485.png" alt=3D"Pong builder pipeline =E2=80=94 eve=
ry node is an LLM call" width=3D"1200" height=3D"137" loading=3D"lazy" deco=
ding=3D"async"></picture></a><figcaption>Pong builder pipeline =E2=80=94 ev=
ery node is an LLM call</figcaption></figure><pre tabindex=3D"0" style=3D"p=
osition: relative;"><code class=3D"language-dot" data-lang=3D"dot">digraph =
build_pong {
    graph [
        goal=3D"Build a two-player Pong TUI game in Go",
        retry_target=3D"implement",
        default_max_retry=3D3,
        model_stylesheet=3D"
            * { llm_model: claude-sonnet-4-5; llm_provider: anthropic; }
            .code { llm_model: claude-opus-4-6; llm_provider: anthropic; }
        "
    ]

    start [shape=3DMdiamond]
    done  [shape=3DMsquare]

    plan      [label=3D"Plan",      class=3D"planning", prompt=3D"Plan the =
architecture..."]
    scaffold  [label=3D"Scaffold",  class=3D"code",     prompt=3D"Initializ=
e Go module..."]
    implement [label=3D"Implement", class=3D"code",     prompt=3D"Write the=
 full game...",
               goal_gate=3Dtrue, max_retries=3D3]
    compile   [label=3D"Compile",   class=3D"code",     prompt=3D"Run go bu=
ild and go vet..."]
    compile_ok [shape=3Ddiamond, label=3D"Compiles?"]
    review    [label=3D"Review",    class=3D"review",   prompt=3D"Review al=
l generated code..."]

    start -&gt; plan -&gt; scaffold -&gt; implement -&gt; compile -&gt; com=
pile_ok
    compile_ok -&gt; review    [label=3D"Pass", condition=3D"outcome=3Dsucc=
ess"]
    compile_ok -&gt; implement [label=3D"Fail", condition=3D"outcome=3Dfail=
"]
    review -&gt; done          [label=3D"Pass", condition=3D"outcome=3Dsucc=
ess"]
}
</code><button class=3D"copy-btn" data-tinylytics-event=3D"code.copy" aria-=
label=3D"Copy code"><svg width=3D"14" height=3D"14" viewBox=3D"0 0 24 24" f=
ill=3D"none" stroke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=3D"2"><rect x=3D"9" y=3D"=
9" width=3D"13" height=3D"13" rx=3D"2" ry=3D"2"></rect><path d=3D"M5 15H4a2=
 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"></path></svg></button></pre=
><p>This style is a build recipe. It leans on LLMs for every step =E2=80=94=
 planning, scaffolding, implementation, review. There=E2=80=99s a <code>mod=
el_stylesheet</code> that maps CSS-like selectors to providers, which is cl=
ever as hell. It=E2=80=99s also expensive, slow, and nondeterministic.</p><=
p>We=E2=80=99ve come to prefer the first style. Tool nodes with shell comma=
nds for anything that can be deterministic. LLM nodes only where you actual=
ly need reasoning. The vulnerability analyzer runs in seconds and costs not=
hing. The Pong builder might take 20 minutes and $15 in API calls, and you =
won=E2=80=99t get the same game twice. Guess which one I want to run at 2am=
 from my phone.</p><p>The most interesting pipelines combine both: determin=
istic tool nodes for setup, validation, and deployment, with LLM nodes only=
 at the points where you genuinely need a model to think. Tracker=E2=80=99s=
 sprint execution pipeline (<a href=3D"https://github.com/2389-research/tra=
cker/blob/main/examples/sprint_exec.dot"><code>sprint_exec.dot</code></a>) =
does this =E2=80=94 shell scripts for ledger management and build validatio=
n, LLM nodes for implementation and review, with three models critiquing ea=
ch other=E2=80=99s reviews in parallel fan-out before a final synthesis dec=
ides whether to ship or loop back.</p><figure><a href=3D"https://2389.ai/po=
sts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/sprint-exec.png"><picture><source=
 type=3D"image/avif" srcset=3D"/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs=
/sprint-exec_hu_4b78866c95199e29.png 600w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot=
-file/graphs/sprint-exec_hu_39e264f071b3ea3a.png 900w, /posts/the-dark-fact=
ory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/sprint-exec_hu_c4c203400525751f.png 1200w" sizes=
=3D"min(100vw, 800px)"><source type=3D"image/webp" srcset=3D"/posts/the-dar=
k-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/sprint-exec_hu_6a81896bfcc1260d.webp 600w, /=
posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/sprint-exec_hu_958683722552b4ea=
.webp 900w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/sprint-exec_hu_b84=
eff510b6ff451.webp 1200w" sizes=3D"min(100vw, 800px)"><img src=3D"https://2=
389.ai/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/sprint-exec_hu_b2ff8aa88=
743a862.png" alt=3D"Sprint execution pipeline =E2=80=94 deterministic tool =
nodes (green) mixed with LLM nodes (purple), multi-model review fan-out wit=
h cross-critique" width=3D"1200" height=3D"1193" loading=3D"lazy" decoding=
=3D"async"></picture></a><figcaption>Sprint execution pipeline =E2=80=94 de=
terministic tool nodes (green) mixed with LLM nodes (purple), multi-model r=
eview fan-out with cross-critique</figcaption></figure><p>And then there=E2=
=80=99s <a href=3D"https://github.com/2389-research/dotpowers/blob/main/dot=
powers.dot"><code>dotpowers.dot</code></a> =E2=80=94 our attempt to clone <=
a href=3D"https://blog.fsck.com/">Jesse=E2=80=99s</a> <a href=3D"https://gi=
thub.com/obra/superpowers">Superpowers</a> into a DOT file. The goal is to =
encode an entire software development lifecycle into a single DOT file. 53 =
nodes across 7 phases: brainstorm with a human, write a design brief, draft=
 and audit a plan, set up a project, implement tasks in a TDD loop with esc=
alation paths, run multi-model review with cross-critique, and finish by me=
rging, creating a PR, or discarding. Human gates at every decision point. T=
hree different LLM providers doing adversarial review. Retry budgets so the=
 pipeline fails gracefully instead of looping forever.</p><figure><a href=
=3D"https://2389.ai/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers.p=
ng"><picture><source type=3D"image/avif" srcset=3D"/posts/the-dark-factory-=
is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers_hu_9624c8b852d009a0.png 600w, /posts/the-dar=
k-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers_hu_ad21e7d982b832cb.png 900w, /pos=
ts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers_hu_94b6c9ce8babe5b6.png =
1200w" sizes=3D"min(100vw, 800px)"><source type=3D"image/webp" srcset=3D"/p=
osts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers_hu_705c22218b90d96.web=
p 600w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers_hu_217fa1b41=
08a2b02.webp 900w, /posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers_h=
u_5c48962b4c9c3339.webp 1200w" sizes=3D"min(100vw, 800px)"><img src=3D"http=
s://2389.ai/posts/the-dark-factory-is-a-dot-file/graphs/dotpowers_hu_4d92d1=
dcc38c1ee4.png" alt=3D"dotpowers =E2=80=94 the full software development li=
fecycle as a single pipeline graph (53 nodes, 7 phases)" width=3D"1200" hei=
ght=3D"1863" loading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async"></picture></a><figcaption>=
dotpowers =E2=80=94 the full software development lifecycle as a single pip=
eline graph (53 nodes, 7 phases)</figcaption></figure><p>One file. Standard=
 DOT syntax. Runs on Mammoth. It=E2=80=99s the kind of thing that only make=
s sense once you stop thinking of the pipeline as a script and start thinki=
ng of it as a process definition. Less shell script, more BPMN diagram. It=
=E2=80=99s weird. I kind of love it.</p><h2 id=3D"share-your-dot-files">Sha=
re your dot files</h2><p>The factory code is dorodango =E2=80=94 polish it,=
 throw it away, rebuild from spec. The pipeline files are the durable artif=
act. They=E2=80=99re the part worth sharing.</p><p>So share them! What does=
 your =E2=80=9Caudit a Rails app=E2=80=9D pipeline look like? Your =E2=80=
=9Conboard a new engineer=E2=80=9D graph? Your =E2=80=9Cship a mobile relea=
se=E2=80=9D DAG? Drop your <code>.dot</code> files in a gist, post them on =
your blog, open a PR somewhere. The dark factory pattern is real, it=E2=80=
=99s reproducible, and agents can build the factory from spec.</p><p>The qu=
estion isn=E2=80=99t how to build the factory anymore. It=E2=80=99s what to=
 build with it.</p></div><footer class=3D"post-entry__footer"><div class=3D=
